# TWO images build from this directory: the control side's (./Dockerfile) and
# the station's (station/bash/docker/Dockerfile). The station image plants the
# station payload with bootstrap.sh and builds heliograph-seal from source, so
# what is NOT sent is a security question rather than a tidiness one: whatever
# arrives here can be copied into a PUBLISHED image.
#
# THE DANGEROUS ONES ARE THE FILES GIT ALREADY HIDES. `.git-token`, `*.key`,
# `*.pem` and `.env` are in the repository's .gitignore precisely because they
# hold credentials, which means they never show up in `git status` and a
# developer working in station/bash/ can have any of them sitting there without
# noticing. bootstrap.sh copies every regular file it finds, so without these
# lines a local token would be planted into the payload and pushed to a public
# registry.
.env
*.local
*.key
*.pem
.git-token
**/.git-token
*credential*
*secret*.txt
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json

# .terraform is the one that matters for SIZE, and bootstrap.sh already
# documents why: `terraform init` drops a provider binary next to each Azure
# template, and azurerm alone is over 300MB. A bootstrapped repo went from about
# 200KB to 913MB before that prune existed. bootstrap.sh prunes it when it
# COPIES, but the docker build would still have uploaded every byte as context.
# .terraform ONLY, not the lock file beside it.
#
# `.terraform.lock.hcl` is a pinned lock and it SHIPS: the control image embeds
# station/bash with `go:embed all:bash`, and excluding the locks here would make
# `heliograph bootstrap` from that image plant a payload whose Azure templates
# re-resolve their provider versions instead of carrying the repository's pins -
# silently different from what the released binary and bootstrap.sh plant.
**/.terraform
**/terraform.tfstate*

**/.git
**/node_modules
site/dist
site/.astro

# Build artefacts of a developer's own machine. A .pyc records the ABSOLUTE
# path it was compiled from, so one that reaches the payload puts somebody's
# worktree path into a published image - and makes the image not derivable from
# the commit. station/bash/azure/function/ has one right now.
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# Captured logs and local station state. A build context is not where anybody's
# evidence should end up, and none of it belongs in a published image.
#
# NARROW ON PURPOSE. `**/secrets/*` was here and it was wrong:
# station/bash/secrets/ is PART OF THE PAYLOAD - it ships a README explaining
# the secret store - and excluding it made the image plant a different payload
# from bootstrap.sh, which nothing would have noticed. The credential patterns
# above catch actual secrets by shape; a whole shipped directory is not one.
**/ops-logs/*.txt
**/.station-*
**/.agent-*
